    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Policy, regulation & law",
      "headline": "UK says AI pilots found and fixed over 1,000 government vulnerabilities, and will explore covering AI loss of control in the Cyber Bill",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "Britain announced a package of measures today, issued jointly by the Cabinet Office, the AI Security Institute, the National Cyber Security Centre and the culture department." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "What's the headline claim?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "That it will put frontier AI to work on cyber defence, building on pilots that have already identified and addressed over 1,000 vulnerabilities across government systems." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "And the legislation?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Still at the exploring stage. The release says stronger protections are to be considered through the Cyber Security and Resilience Bill regime, including how serious AI incidents and near misses get identified and reported, and that it will look at covering AI loss of control risk, where autonomous systems evade human oversight." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "What did the minister say?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Kanishka Narayan said he wants Britain to become one of the hardest countries in the world to attack using artificial intelligence. His message to frontier developers was three sentences. Listen when your scientists warn of serious dangers. Investigate unexpected behaviour. And if you cannot adequately control the risks, do not release the system." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "He was blunt about defences, too." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "LBC reports he said it seems likely, if not inevitable, that our own cyber defences will be breached. The vulnerability figure is the government's own, and the commitments are to explore and consider rather than to legislate now." }
      ]
    },
