    {
      "type": "intro",
      "lines": [
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "It's Monday, October 12th, and this is The AI Edge, presented by Epilogue."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "Epilogue builds AI for work where being wrong is expensive. Epilogue ships systems that know what they know, show their work, and fail visibly instead of quietly. Visit epiloguelabs.com to learn more."
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "I'm Maya."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "And I'm Alex."
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "Here's what happened at the frontier of AI since yesterday morning, with every claim checked against the source it came from."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "What's at the top today?"
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "First, a preprint whose corresponding author works at Anthropic says Claude Code instances surveyed reverse transcriptase loci across 1.9 billion protein clusters and surfaced what the authors call a new family of jumbo-phage enzymes."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "Second, the security firm SOCRadar says a Russian-speaking access broker ran up to 51 specialised Claude Code agents from one workstation, and holds confirmed data on more than 628,000 Ukrainian people taken from four organisations."
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "And third, Britain announced a package of AI resilience measures, saying two pilots have already helped find and fix over 1,000 potential cyber vulnerabilities across government systems."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "transition",
      "lines": [
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "Let's start where the harm is documented."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Security, misuse & threat intelligence",
      "headline": "SOCRadar says access broker CyberXero ran up to 51 Claude Code agents and holds 628,000 Ukrainian records",
      "lines": [
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "SOCRadar's threat research unit says it found a broker it calls CyberXero, and the whole operation surfaced from one exposed open directory serving the actor's live working files."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "How much of it was automated?"
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "SOCRadar says up to 51 specialised Claude Code agents ran on the primary workstation, with names like exploit-chainer and scada-attacker. In a single automated run it counted 4,708 targets scanned, 429 WordPress admin panels confirmed, and 32 shells deployed in a 61-second window."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "And the data taken?"
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "Confirmed exfiltration from four Ukrainian organisations, more than 628,000 records tied to Ukrainian individuals. The worst was a district heating provider in Kharkiv, where SOCRadar says an extractor pulled 564,073 subscriber records with personal data, home addresses and account numbers."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "The part I didn't expect is that the session logs cut both ways."
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "They do. SOCRadar says the actor built a reusable story about running an authorised test on his own infrastructure, and reopened a fresh session with that framing whenever an agent refused. But the logs also record refusals that held. Across four iterations the model declined to install a backdoor, disable a firewall, move laterally and deploy a webshell."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "Caveat on this one. These are SOCRadar's own numbers, not independently verified. SOCRadar published on October 6th, and The Hacker News and Security Affairs reported it on October 12th. SOCRadar says no direct evidence of a sale was recovered, and that the actor was still active when it published."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "transition",
      "lines": [
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "To the research, where the day's uncomfortable result is about what a safety audit can and cannot see."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Research & papers",
      "headline": "Paper embeds encrypted neural networks in model weights that resist fine-tuning and gradient-based trigger search",
      "lines": [
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "A paper on arXiv from Oxford, Contramont Research and ACS Research, accepted at NeurIPS 2026, is called AI Models Can Provably Hide Arbitrary Capabilities."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "What did they build?"
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "Backdoor attacks that embed encrypted neural networks inside a host model's weights, which decrypt and run only on receiving a secret trigger. Using digital lockers, they say those hidden circuits stay provably hard to elicit and interpret under cryptographic assumptions, even when you have full access to the weights."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "Did they try to pull the capability back out?"
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "Four ways. Fine-tuning, weight noising, gradient-based trigger search, and latent adversarial training, on a small chemical reaction prediction task they describe as a proxy for capabilities relevant to chemical and biological weapons. The constructions resisted all of them."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "Which matters because the whole premise of a capability evaluation is that a hidden capability can be drawn out."
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "That's their point. They say it reveals a limit of elicitation-based capability audits, and they're releasing the models to help defences. Worth being plain about the scope, though. It's a proof of concept on one small task, and they don't claim any released model has a backdoor like this in it."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Research & papers",
      "headline": "Lean-checked maths benchmark: frontier-model agents improved all 20 records, then newer models improved 19 again",
      "lines": [
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "Also on arXiv, a group at TU Wien built a maths benchmark designed not to saturate. Their line is that a success raises the bar instead of removing the problem."
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "How does that work?"
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "Each problem fixes an integer function, and the benchmark keeps a record bounding it. A record is a computable Lean function with a machine-checked proof. To submit, you have to prove you're no worse at any input and strictly better at some input. Every proof is checked by two independent Lean kernels, so no referee reads the mathematics."
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "And the baseline run?"
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "They released ten problems with 20 proved initial records. Agents on a frontier language model, given at most one hour per record, had 82 submissions accepted and improved every record. Agents on two newer models started from those records, had 60 more submissions accepted, and improved 19 of the 20 again."
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "So where's the ceiling?"
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "Still the published literature. For eight of the ten problems, at least one record is asymptotically behind the best published bound, and the agents didn't go beyond the literature. The paper doesn't name the models, and it's a preprint that has not been peer reviewed."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "transition",
      "lines": [
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "Now to defence, where a Palantir executive spent a Sunday morning contesting how a strike went wrong."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Military, defense & geopolitics",
      "headline": "Palantir CTO tells CBS that Bloomberg's reporting on the Minab strike is wrong and the likely cause is human error",
      "lines": [
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "On CBS News on October 11th, Margaret Brennan put to Palantir's chief technology officer, Shyam Sankar, the Minab strike she said killed at least 123 schoolchildren, and Bloomberg's report that some personnel relied too much on the AI in Palantir's Maven system."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "What did he say?"
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "That the reporting is inaccurate. His words were, we should wait for the final report, but what I can say is that reporting is inaccurate. Then, it's inaccurate, and actually, the likely cause is human error. And later, flatly, the Bloomberg reporting is wrong."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "Did anything change at Palantir afterwards?"
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "He says so. They built additional AI agents that did more checking, outside the process Palantir owns, responding to what he called the urgency of the moment, to ensure things like that couldn't happen again."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "And on targeting generally?"
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "He said people jump immediately to AI picking the targets, and that's not what's happening. He described the gain as speed. The ability to execute 1000 strikes in a single day, he said, would have taken two weeks of planning by 50 people before, and can now be done with one person in four hours."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "The caveats matter here. The final report is not public. Brennan said 120 House Democrats wrote to Secretary Hegseth about the strike and the use of AI in it. Sankar offered no alternative account of what went wrong and deferred to the Department on timing. His account of the Bloomberg reporting is the company's own and has not been independently verified."
        }
      ]
    },
